  { q: 'Do ravens really mate for life?', a: 'Ravens are socially monogamous and usually maintain long-term pair bonds — often for many years — but "for life" is not accurate for all individuals. Divorce (pair separation while both partners are still alive) has been documented, though it is uncommon. Widowed birds do re-pair. The bond is better described as long-term and stable than as absolute.' },
  { q: 'How do ravens choose a mate?', a: 'Pair formation in ravens involves extended courtship — aerial displays, mutual preening (allopreening), bill contact (billing), and vocalisation. Young ravens in non-breeding flocks form loose associations and "trial" relationships before committing to a territory and a mate. Females appear to prefer males that demonstrate territorial competence and reliable food access.' },
